      {
        "id": "def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Particle spacetime, observers and worldlines",
        "statement": "Use the signature $(-,+,+,+)$ throughout. In the flat case, **Minkowski spacetime** means the affine model $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ of [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]]: $A$ is an affine space, $\\eta$ is a nondegenerate Lorentz form on its four-dimensional displacement space, and $C^+$ selects the future cone. In the curved case, a **background spacetime** means a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold $(M,g,N)$ of [[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]; the metric and time orientation are fixed data, not dynamical unknowns here. No field equation or completeness assumption is included.\n\nA **massive worldline** on an open interval $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ is a $C^2$ curve $x:I\\to M$ with future-directed timelike tangent $T=dx/ds$ at every parameter value. This is the smooth subclass of [[def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time]]. A **null worldline** is a $C^2$ curve with nonzero future-directed null tangent everywhere. Its tangent has no proper-time normalization: $g(T,T)=0$, so the massive proper-time formula does not apply. In the flat model these are affine curves into $A$ with tangent in the selected future timelike or null cone from [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]].\n\nAn **observer at an event** $p\\in M$ is a future unit timelike vector $n\\in T_pM$ with $g_p(n,n)=-1$, as in [[def-gr-observer-and-rest-space]]. Its instantaneous rest space is $n^\\perp=\\{v:g_p(v,n)=0\\}$. This is a pointwise vector-space construction; the definition does not assign a clock reading or a moving frame to a lone observer.\n\nAn **observer congruence** on an open set $U\\subseteq M$ is a smooth field of future unit timelike vectors on $U$, as in [[def-gr-observer-congruence-and-adapted-charts]]; its observer curves are its integral curves. A **tetrad** is a chosen orthonormal frame field, as in [[def-gr-local-frame-and-tetrad-fields]]. A **chart** is a coordinate map; an adapted chart is a chart whose spatial coordinates are constant along the congruence's integral curves. These objects are distinct: a chart need not be inertial, a tetrad need not be coordinate induced, and a congruence is a field of observers rather than a chart or a frame. This distinction agrees with [[def-sr-chart-tetrad-and-congruence-distinction]].\n\nThe coordinates and units used later are SI: in an inertial chart $x^0=ct$ and $x^i$ are metres, $t$ is seconds, $c>0$ is metres per second, and proper time is measured in seconds. In a general chart, coordinate components inherit their coordinate units; invariant equations are tensorial. No global inertial chart, global tetrad, or global observer congruence is asserted to exist.",
        "deps": [
          "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
          "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects",
          "def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time",
          "def-gr-observer-and-rest-space",
