      {
        "id": "lem-rpm-shell-tangent-force-ivp",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Smooth shell-tangent force initial-value problem",
        "statement": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be a smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold and let $m,c>0$. Let $\\mathcal V\\subset TM$ be an open set of future timelike vectors and let $f$ be a smooth vector field along the bundle projection $\\mathcal V\\to M$, with $f(p,V)\\in T_pM$ and $g_p(V,f(p,V))=0$ for every $(p,V)\\in\\mathcal V$. Fix an initial parameter $s_0\\in\\mathbb R$. For every initial datum $(p_0,U_0)\\in\\mathcal V$ satisfying $g_{p_0}(U_0,U_0)=-c^2$, there is an open interval $J\\ni s_0$ and a unique local solution consisting of a $C^2$ curve $x:J\\to M$ and its $C^1$ tangent field $U=dx/ds$, with $x(s_0)=p_0$, $U(s_0)=U_0$, $dx/ds=U$, and $D_sU=f(x,U)/m$. The solution depends smoothly on its initial data and on any finite-dimensional parameters on which $f$ depends smoothly, on a common sufficiently short compact interval containing $s_0$ near the base datum. It remains on the future unit timelike shell. There is a unique maximal solution on an open interval containing $s_0$, but this interval need not be all of $\\mathbb R$.",
        "deps": [
          "def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline",
          "thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative",
          "lem-gr-parallel-transport-and-geodesic-speed",
          "thm-picard-lindelof-local-existence-and-uniqueness",
