      {
        "id": "post-rpm-test-clock-and-background",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Ideal clocks on a prescribed Lorentzian background",
        "statement": "**Adopted convention.** On a prescribed background $(M,g,N)$ from [[def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline]], let $x:[a,b]\\to M$, $a<b$, be a continuous piecewise-$C^1$ future timelike curve in the sense of [[def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time]]: it has a finite partition $a=s_0<\\cdots<s_k=b$, each segment is $C^1$ up to its endpoints with future timelike one-sided tangents there, and the two tangents at a corner need not agree. If $s$ denotes its parameter and $T_i=dx/ds$ on the $i$th segment, an ideal clock carried along it records the increment\n\n$$\\Delta\\tau=\\frac1c\\sum_{i=1}^k\\int_{s_{i-1}}^{s_i}\\sqrt{-g(T_i,T_i)}\\,ds.$$\n\nThe increment is independent of the admissible finite partition, by additivity of the integral, and is additive across corners. On each smooth segment this is the proper-time parameter with $d\\tau=c^{-1}\\sqrt{-g(dx/ds,dx/ds)}\\,ds$. The flat-spacetime specialization is inherited from [[post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light]]; extending that convention to the curved prescribed background is a separate adoption of this framework.\n\nWhen the parameter $s$ is measured in seconds, $x^0=ct$ is in metres, $c$ is in metres per second, and $\\tau$ is in seconds. This is a model postulate, not a theorem or a reported measurement. It does not assert that a physical clock realizes the idealization exactly, that the background solves gravitational field equations, or that the test body affects the background.",
        "deps": [
          "def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline",
          "def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time",
          "post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, General Relativity, author-hosted lecture-note chapter excerpt, \u00a71.1.1 particle worldline action and \u00a71.2 proper time",
              "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/general-relativity/gr1.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Sean Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, complete lecture notes, Ch. 1",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
            }
          ]
        },
        "physical_scope": "Adopted classical test-particle framework on a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold; the curved-background clock reading convention extends the flat ideal-clock postulate and is not an empirical accuracy claim.",
        "library": "physics"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-rpm-length-action-first-variation",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Timelike length action: arbitrary-parameter first variation",
        "statement": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold, $a<b$, and $x:[a,b]\\to M$ a $C^2$ future timelike curve. Put $T=dx/ds$, $\\ell=\\sqrt{-g(T,T)}>0$, and for a constant $m>0$ and $c>0$ define the length action $S[x]=-mc\\int_a^b\\ell(s)\\,ds$. Let $H(\\epsilon,s)$ be a $C^2$ fixed-endpoint variation through future timelike curves for $\\epsilon$ near $0$, with $H(0,s)=x(s)$, $H(\\epsilon,a)=x(a)$, $H(\\epsilon,b)=x(b)$, and variation field $J=\\partial_\\epsilon H|_0$. Then\n\n$$\\delta S=\\left.\\frac{d}{d\\epsilon}S[H(\\epsilon,\\cdot)]\\right|_{0}=-mc\\int_a^b g\\!\\left(J,D_s\\!\\left(\\frac{T}{\\ell}\\right)\\right)ds.$$\n\nConsequently $x$ is stationary under all such variations exactly when $D_s(T/\\ell)=0$, equivalently $D_\\tau U=0$ for the proper-time parameter of [[thm-rpm-proper-time-parametrization]]. The length integral has the same value under every orientation-preserving $C^1$ change of variable $s=\\phi(r)$ with $\\phi:[a',b']\\to[a,b]$ a diffeomorphism and $\\phi'>0$, namely $\\int_{a'}^{b'}\\ell_r(r)\\,dr=\\int_a^b\\ell(s)\\,ds$ for $T_r(r)=\\phi'(r)T(\\phi(r))$ and $\\ell_r(r)=\\sqrt{-g(T_r(r),T_r(r))}$. As a transformation of the stated $C^2$ curve and variation class, require instead that $\\phi$ be an orientation-preserving $C^2$ diffeomorphism; then the stationarity equation is covariant, since $D_r(T_r/\\ell_r)(r)=\\phi'(r)(D_s(T/\\ell))(\\phi(r))$. This is a local variational statement; it makes no claim about global proper-time maximality, and the square-root action is not defined for a nonzero null tangent.",
        "deps": [
          "def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline",
          "thm-rpm-proper-time-parametrization",
          "thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative",
