      {
        "id": "post-rpm-massive-test-particle-action",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Massive spinless test-particle action",
        "statement": "Let $a,b\\in\\mathbb R$ with $a<b$, and let $x:[a,b]\\to M$ be a $C^2$ future timelike curve on the prescribed background of [[post-rpm-test-clock-and-background]]. Let $H:(-\\epsilon_0,\\epsilon_0)\\times[a,b]\\to M$ be a $C^2$ variation, for some $\\epsilon_0>0$, such that each $H(\\epsilon,\\cdot)$ is future timelike, $H(0,s)=x(s)$, and $H(\\epsilon,a)=x(a)$ and $H(\\epsilon,b)=x(b)$. For a spinless, structureless massive test body of constant primitive mass $m>0$, measured in kilograms, and the SI conversion constant $c>0$ metres per second, adopt the action on this finite segment\n\n$$S_m[x]=-mc\\int_a^b\\sqrt{-g(dx/ds,dx/ds)}\\,ds=-mc^2\\Delta\\tau,$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta\\tau:=c^{-1}\\int_a^b\\sqrt{-g(dx/ds,dx/ds)}\\,ds$ is the ideal-clock increment specified by [[post-rpm-test-clock-and-background]] on this smooth segment. The model's free trajectories are precisely the curves stationary under the fixed-endpoint $C^2$ variations just specified; this is the local variation class in [[lem-rpm-length-action-first-variation]]. When $s$ is measured in seconds, $T$ has units metres per second, and the action has units joule-seconds. This is an adopted model premise, not a consequence of the metric or clock postulate. It excludes self-gravity, radiation reaction, spin, finite-size structure, particle creation, and quantum effects; it does not assert empirical adequacy for any particular body.",
        "deps": [
          "post-rpm-test-clock-and-background",
          "thm-rpm-proper-time-parametrization",
          "lem-rpm-length-action-first-variation"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "post-rpm-test-clock-and-background": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "thm-rpm-proper-time-parametrization": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "lem-rpm-length-action-first-variation": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, General Relativity, author-hosted lecture-note chapter excerpt, \u00a71.1.1 particle action",
