      {
        "id": "post-rpm-null-test-particle-action",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Massless test-particle action and momentum scale",
        "statement": "Let $a,b\\in\\mathbb R$ with $a<b$. A configuration on $[a,b]$ consists of a $C^2$ curve $x:[a,b]\\to M$ and a smooth positive function $e:[a,b]\\to(0,\\infty)$ on the prescribed background of [[post-rpm-test-clock-and-background]]. Vary $x$ and $e$ independently: $x$ through $C^2$ variations $H:(-\\epsilon_0,\\epsilon_0)\\times[a,b]\\to M$ for some $\\epsilon_0>0$, with $H(0,s)=x(s)$ and $H(\\epsilon,a)=x(a)$, $H(\\epsilon,b)=x(b)$, and no causal restriction on the varied curves; and $e$ through jointly smooth positive variations $e_\\epsilon:[a,b]\\to(0,\\infty)$ whose variation field $\\delta e:=\\partial_\\epsilon e_\\epsilon|_{\\epsilon=0}$ is compactly supported in $(a,b)$. Adopt a distinct massless test-particle sector with primitive action\n\n$$S_0[x,e]=\\frac12\\int_a^b e(s)^{-1}g_{x(s)}(T(s),T(s))\\,ds,\\qquad T(s)=dx/ds,$$\n\nand stationary under all such independent variations. The variational equations and their null branch follow from [[lem-rpm-einbein-variation-and-equivalence]]: the constraint is $g(T,T)=0$, and the physical branch requires $T$ to be nonzero and future-directed. The associated momentum $P=T/e$ is nonzero future null; its positive scale is primitive initial data, not $mU$ for any $m=0$ substitution. Null curves have no proper-time parameter. With $s$ in seconds, $T$ in metres per second, and $e$ in kg$^{-1}$, the integral has units joule-seconds. For finite intervals $[a',b']$ and $[a,b]$, an orientation-preserving $C^\\infty$ diffeomorphism $s=\\phi(r):[a',b']\\to[a,b]$ with $\\phi'>0$ transforms $T_r(r)=\\phi'(r)T(\\phi(r))$ and $e_r(r)=\\phi'(r)e(\\phi(r))$; this leaves the functional invariant by [[lem-rpm-einbein-variation-and-equivalence]] and preserves the stated configuration class. Neither the scale nor a clock reading is inferred from the ray image alone.\n\nThis is an adopted classical model formulation, not an empirical observation or a theorem. Its scope excludes mass generation, interactions, and quantum field effects.",
        "deps": [
          "post-rpm-test-clock-and-background",
          "lem-rpm-einbein-variation-and-equivalence"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "post-rpm-test-clock-and-background": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "lem-rpm-einbein-variation-and-equivalence": "formulation-prerequisite"
