      {
        "id": "def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Mechanical momentum and observer energy",
        "statement": "Use the massive and null sectors as separately adopted in [[post-rpm-massive-test-particle-action]] and [[post-rpm-null-test-particle-action]]. For a massive body of constant mass $m>0$, its **mechanical four-momentum** is $P=mU$, where $U$ is its future unit tangent with $g(U,U)=-c^2$. In the null sector, the mechanical four-momentum is the separately specified nonzero future-null vector $P$ from the einbein formulation; no null proper time or $P=mU$ is defined.\n\nAt an event, for any observer $n$ of [[def-gr-observer-and-rest-space]], define the spatial momentum and observer energy by\n\n$$q_n(P):=P+g(P,n)n\\in n^\\perp,\\qquad E_n(P):=-c\\,g(P,n).$$\n\nThe projection identity and positive-definiteness of $n^\\perp$ are supplied by [[lem-gr-observer-projection-decomposition]]. With SI units, $P$ has units kg m s$^{-1}$, $q_n$ has the same units, and $E_n$ has units joules. This definition is pointwise and does not assert conservation, positivity, or an energy interpretation for an arbitrary vector field that is not an observer.",
        "deps": [
          "post-rpm-massive-test-particle-action",
          "post-rpm-null-test-particle-action",
          "def-gr-observer-and-rest-space",
          "lem-gr-observer-projection-decomposition"
