              "title": "Sean Carroll, Lecture Notes on General Relativity, complete lecture notes, Ch. 1 four-force",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/9712019"
            },
            {
              "title": "David Tong, General Relativity, author-hosted lecture-note chapter excerpt, \u00a71.1.1 particle motion",
              "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/general-relativity/gr1.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "physical_scope": "A selected smooth external force law for a named massive test body of constant mass on a prescribed background; orthogonality is a compatibility condition preserving the mass shell, not a universal force principle.",
        "library": "physics"
      },
      {
        "id": "pthm-rpm-killing-particle-charge",
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Killing particle charge and force balance",
        "statement": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq M$ be an open region of the prescribed background and let $K$ be a smooth Killing field on $\\Omega$. For a massive force-postulate trajectory $x:I\\to M$, parameterized by proper time on a nonempty open interval $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ with $x(I)\\subseteq\\Omega$, constant $m>0$, $P=mU$, and $D_\\tau P=f$, the scalar $g(K,P)$ obeys\n\n$$\\frac{d}{d\\tau}g(K,P)=g(K,f).$$\n\nThus it is constant when the body is free ($f=0$). For a free null geodesic $y:J\\to M$ on a nonempty open interval $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$, with $y(J)\\subseteq\\Omega$, affine parameter $\\lambda$, and tangent momentum $P=dy/d\\lambda$, $g(K,P)$ is constant along $\\lambda$ as well. If $K$ is a future unit timelike observer field along the applicable curve, then $-c\\,g(K,P)$ has energy units and is the energy defined by that observer; for a general Killing field it is only a conserved scalar, not automatically an energy.",
        "deps": [
          "def-gr-killing-field",
          "thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative",
          "lem-gr-connection-conditions-in-chart-components",
